{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of quiet that only happens at 4 a.m. It's not empty. It's full. \n\nFull of the hum of the refrigerator, the tick of a cooling house, the occasional car passing two streets over, its headlights sweeping across the ceiling like a slow comet. \n\nI used to think creativity needed noise, coffee shops, chatter, playlists engineered for focus. Turns out my best ideas arrive when the world has logged off. \n\nThey slip in quietly, not demanding attention. An idea about a story where memories are stored in jars. A solution to a problem I'd been chewing on for three days. The sudden urge to learn how to make sourdough, or whittle. \n\nI keep a notebook by my bed now, not for dreams, though it catches those too, but for these 4 a.m. visitors. Most of them are terrible in daylight. Half-baked, pretentious, impossible. But every tenth one glows. \n\nAnd that's enough. You don't need a hundred good ideas. You need one that sticks around after the sun comes up, that still looks good with the kitchen lights on. You need one that makes you get out of bed. \n\nI think we all have a 4 a.m. inside us, even at noon. A quiet layer underneath the notifications and errands. If you can find a way to listen to it for five minutes a day, you win. \n\nNot forever, just long enough to remember who you are when no one is watching or asking for anything. That is freedom. quiet freedom.",
